Given: Length of line AB, [latex]L = 100\ \text{m}[/latex] Whole Circle Bearing (WCB) of AB, [latex]\theta = 150^\circ[/latex] Step 1: Determine the quadrant A WCB of [latex]150^\circ[/latex] lies between [latex]90^\circ[/latex] and [latex]180^\circ[/latex]. Therefore, the line lies in the South-East (SE) quadrant. Hence: Latitude = Negative (South) Departure = Positive (East) Step 2: Calculate Latitude Latitude is given by: [latex]
\text{Latitude} = L\cos\theta = 100\cos 150^\circ  =100(-0.8660)
=-86.60\ \text{m}
[/latex] Step 3: Calculate Departure Departure is given by: [latex]
\text{Departure} = L\sin\theta = 100\sin 150^\circ =100(0.5)=50.00\ \text{m}
[/latex]

Given: Permeability corresponding to void ratio [latex]e_1=0.60[/latex] is

[latex]
k_1=0.14\ \text{cm/s}
[/latex] The required void ratio is

[latex]
e_2=0.80
[/latex] Using Taylor's equation for permeability:

[latex]
k \propto \dfrac{e^3}{1+e}
[/latex] Therefore, [latex]
\dfrac{k_2}{k_1}
=
\frac{\dfrac{e_2^3}{1+e_2}}
{\dfrac{e_1^3}{1+e_1}}
[/latex] Substituting the given values: [latex]
\begin{aligned}
k_2
&=0.14\left(\dfrac{\dfrac{(0.80)^3}{1+0.80}}
{\dfrac{(0.60)^3}{1+0.60}}\right)\\[4pt]
&=0.14\left(\dfrac{\dfrac{0.512}{1.8}}
{\dfrac{0.216}{1.6}}\right)\\[4pt]
&=0.14\left(\dfrac{0.2844}{0.135}\right)\\[4pt]
&=0.14(2.107)\\
&=0.295
\end{aligned}
[/latex]

Key Concept: According to Rankine's earth pressure theory, the failure plane is inclined at [latex]
\boxed{45^\circ+\frac{\phi}{2}}
[/latex]

with respect to the major principal plane . Active Earth Pressure Condition In the active state, the vertical stress is the major principal stress. Therefore, the failure plane makes an angle of [latex]
45^\circ+\dfrac{\phi}{2}
[/latex]

with the major principal plane. Hence: Option (A) is True Option (B) is False Passive Earth Pressure Condition In the passive state, the horizontal stress is the major principal stress. The failure plane still makes an angle of [latex]
45^\circ+\dfrac{\phi}{2}
[/latex]

with the major principal plane. Hence: Option (C) is True Option (D) is False

Step 1: Use the factor of safety equation for an infinite slope For a dry infinite slope, [latex]
F=\dfrac{c'}{\gamma H\sin\beta\cos\beta}
+\dfrac{\tan\phi'}{\tan\beta}
[/latex] At the critical height, the slope is on the verge of failure, hence

[latex]
F=1
[/latex] Therefore,

[latex]
1=\dfrac{c'}{\gamma H_c\sin\beta\cos\beta}
+\dfrac{\tan\phi'}{\tan\beta}
[/latex] Rearranging, [latex]
H_c=
\dfrac{c'}
{\gamma\sin\beta\cos\beta
\left(1-\dfrac{\tan\phi'}{\tan\beta}\right)}
[/latex] Step 2: Substitute the given values [latex]
H_c=
\dfrac{12}
{15.72\times\sin22^\circ\times\cos22^\circ
\left(1-\dfrac{\tan15^\circ}{\tan22^\circ}\right)}
[/latex] [latex]
\begin{aligned}
H_c
&=
\dfrac{12}
{15.72\times0.3746\times0.9272
\left(1-\dfrac{0.2679}{0.4040}\right)}\\
&=
\dfrac{12}
{15.72\times0.3473\times0.3369}\\[4pt]
&=
\dfrac{12}{1.839}\\[4pt]
&=6.52\ \text{m}
\end{aligned}
[/latex] Hence, the critical height of the slope is 
[latex]
H_c=6.52\ \text{m}
[/latex]

Option A: Warping stresses develop in a rigid pavement due to the temperature difference between the top and bottom surfaces of the slab during a day. The temperature gradient causes the slab to curl upward or downward, producing warping stresses. Therefore,

[latex]
\boxed{\text{Option A is TRUE}}
[/latex] Option B: Seasonal changes in temperature cause the entire slab to expand or contract. Since the slab movement is restrained by subgrade friction, frictional stresses are developed. Therefore,

[latex]
\boxed{\text{Option B is TRUE}}
[/latex] Option C: In rigid pavement design, the critical tensile stress is not obtained by taking the maximum of wheel load stress, warping stress, and frictional stress individually. The design stress is generally obtained by combining the wheel load stress and temperature-related stresses at the critical location. Hence, taking only the maximum of the individual stresses is incorrect. Therefore,

[latex]
\boxed{\text{Option C is FALSE}}
[/latex] Option D: Wheel load stresses at edge, interior, and corner do not occur simultaneously at the same location. The critical condition is obtained by considering the most critical loading position, not by summing all three stresses. Therefore,

[latex]
\boxed{\text{Option D is FALSE}}
[/latex]
